      <section class="section">
        <p>A <strong>metered dose valve</strong> is a precision aerosol valve that delivers a fixed, reproducible quantity of product with every actuation — regardless of canister fill level, actuation force, or orientation. It achieves this through a small internal metering chamber whose volume is defined during manufacture, filling on the upstroke and discharging completely on the downstroke. This engineering precision makes metered dose valves the foundation of pharmaceutical inhaler technology, topical drug delivery, and any aerosol application where an inconsistent dose creates a safety, efficacy, or regulatory problem.</p>
        <p>Understanding how metered dose valves are designed, how their dose accuracy is characterised, and what failure modes affect their leak performance is essential knowledge for aerosol formulators, product development engineers, and quality assurance teams working across both pharmaceutical and industrial aerosol sectors. This guide provides a complete technical overview of MDV engineering — covering component function, dose variability mechanisms, gasket compatibility, leak standards, and QC testing protocols.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="section-1" class="section">
        <h2>What Is a Metered Dose Valve?</h2>
        <p>A metered dose valve (MDV) is a subtype of aerosol dispensing valve engineered to deliver a pre-defined unit volume of product per actuation cycle. Unlike continuous spray valves, which allow product to flow for as long as the actuator is depressed, an MDV physically limits each discharge to the volume of its internal metering chamber — typically expressed in microlitres (µL) for pharmaceutical applications or in milligrams (mg) of spray weight for consumer aerosols. The principle is mechanically elegant: the metering chamber fills passively between actuations and is isolated from the bulk formulation at the moment of delivery.</p>
        <p>Metered dose valves are most prominently associated with pressurised Metered Dose Inhalers (pMDIs), where each actuation must deliver a precise drug dose to the patient's airways. However, the same valve class is used in topical pharmaceutical sprays, metered-dose nasal sprays, and certain precision industrial applications. As detailed in our guide to <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2" target="_top">aerosol propellants</a>, the propellant system interacts intimately with valve design — propellant vapour pressure, density, and solvent properties all influence whether the metering chamber fills and empties with the reproducibility that MDV engineering demands.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>MDVs deliver a fixed volume per actuation, independent of canister pressure at time of use</li>
          <li>Standard chamber volumes range from approximately 25 µL to 100 µL in pharmaceutical applications, and up to several hundred µL in consumer metered sprays</li>
          <li>The valve housing, stem, metering chamber body, inlet gasket, and outlet gasket are the five primary subcomponents</li>
          <li>MDVs may be designed for upright, inverted, or any-orientation use depending on dip tube and chamber geometry</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="section-2" class="section">
        <h2>Valve Anatomy &amp; Component Functions</h2>
        <p>The metered dose valve is a multi-component precision assembly in which every element has a defined engineering function. Failure or dimensional drift in any single component propagates directly into delivered dose variability or leak failure — which is why MDV manufacturing tolerances are among the tightest in the consumer goods or pharmaceutical packaging supply chain. Understanding the function of each component is the prerequisite for diagnosing dose inconsistency, selecting compatible materials, and specifying qualification tests. The <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" target="_top">aerosol valve gasket</a> in particular deserves attention as the component most sensitive to formulation chemistry.</p>
        
        <div class="table-responsive" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
