      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="section-3" class="section">
        <h2>Dose Accuracy: Engineering Determinants</h2>
        <p>Dose accuracy in a metered dose valve is the outcome of a tight engineering system — not a single component property. Every dimensional variable in the valve assembly contributes to the statistical distribution of delivered doses measured across an actuation sequence. In pharmaceutical MDI development, regulators expect manufacturers to characterise this distribution through dose uniformity testing at the beginning, middle, and end of canister life, demonstrating that delivered doses remain within an acceptable range throughout the product shelf life. For consumer aerosol products, while regulatory requirements are less stringent, consistent dose weight directly affects consumer perception of product quality.</p>

        <div class="callout" style="border-left: 3px solid #16a34a; background: #f0fdf4; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 1.8em 0; font-size: 0.93rem;">
          <strong style="color: #16a34a; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Key Insight</strong>
          The metering chamber volume is defined by the moulded geometry of the valve body — not adjustable in production. This is why dimensional qualification of the metering chamber at mould validation stage is the single most consequential quality control intervention in MDV manufacturing.
        </div>

        <p>The principal engineering variables that determine dose accuracy include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Metering chamber volume precision:</strong> Injection-moulded polymer chambers carry inherent dimensional variation. Tolerances tighter than ±2% of nominal volume require high-precision tooling and rigorous in-process dimensional monitoring.</li>
          <li><strong>Inlet orifice geometry:</strong> The inlet orifice determines how quickly the metering chamber refills between actuations. If the orifice is too small, rapid repeat actuations may deliver sub-dose volumes because the chamber has not fully refilled.</li>
          <li><strong>Stem travel distance:</strong> The distance the stem travels on actuation must fully open the outlet orifice to allow complete chamber emptying. Insufficient stem travel causes partial discharge — the single most common cause of low-dose actuation failures.</li>
          <li><strong>Gasket sealing integrity:</strong> An inlet gasket that does not seal fully allows bulk formulation to bleed into the metering chamber during discharge, increasing the delivered dose unpredictably. An outlet gasket that does not fully reseal allows pressure leakage between actuations, affecting subsequent dose volumes.</li>
          <li><strong>Formulation viscosity and density:</strong> High-viscosity formulations fill the metering chamber more slowly, making dose volume sensitive to the dwell time between consecutive actuations.</li>
          <li><strong>Temperature effects:</strong> Propellant vapour pressure is temperature-dependent. At higher temperatures, elevated pressure within the can increases the force driving formulation into the metering chamber and affects the thermodynamic state of the formulation at the point of discharge.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>According to <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM International</a>, the test method ASTM D5964 provides a standardised procedure for measuring aerosol spray rate — a commonly used proxy for valve consistency in consumer aerosol quality control, where individual dose weighing per actuation is not routinely feasible.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="section-4" class="section">
        <h2>Leak Performance &amp; Industry Standards</h2>
        <p>Aerosol valve leak performance is a product safety and regulatory compliance issue of the highest importance. A leaking valve compromises product integrity, creates fire and explosion hazards in storage environments, exposes users to propellant inhalation, and results in non-conformance with the regulations that govern aerosol products in every major market. Leak performance in metered dose valves is more demanding than in continuous spray valves because the MDV must maintain a seal at both the inlet and outlet gasket simultaneously throughout the product shelf life — under conditions of sustained propellant pressure, formulation exposure, and cyclic thermal stress.</p>

        <div class="callout" style="border-left: 3px solid #d97706; background: #fffbeb; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 1.8em 0; font-size: 0.93rem;">
          <strong style="color: #d97706; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          For pharmaceutical MDIs, gasket swell of more than approximately 10–15% in the relevant propellant system is generally considered a disqualifying result during material qualification — because excessive swell alters the sealing geometry and changes both leak rate and metering chamber volume in a non-linear and irreversible manner.
        </div>

