      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="section-5" class="section">
        <h2>Gasket Material Selection for Metered Dose Valves</h2>
        <p>The elastomer gaskets within a metered dose valve are the component most vulnerable to chemical attack from the formulation and propellant system. Unlike metallic valve components — which are largely chemically inert — elastomer gaskets can absorb propellant, swell in the presence of organic solvents, harden through plasticiser extraction, or leach compounding agents into the formulation. For pharmaceutical MDIs, gasket materials are regulatory substances of interest because extractables and leachables from elastomers must be characterised and assessed against inhalation toxicological thresholds as required by FDA and EMA guidelines.</p>

        <p>This makes the gasket qualification process one of the most time-consuming elements of MDV product development. As covered in our technical overview of <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" target="_top">aerosol valve gaskets</a>, the interaction between the elastomer and the formulation must be evaluated for dimensional swell, weight change, tensile property retention, and leachables profile before an elastomer grade is released for production use. The <a href="https://echa.europa.eu/regulations/reach" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ECHA REACH regulation</a> also applies to gasket compounding agents used in aerosol products placed on the EU market, adding a chemical registration dimension to material selection.</p>

