        <div class="callout" style="border-left: 3px solid #16a34a; background: #f0fdf4; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 1.8em 0; font-size: 0.93rem;">
          <strong style="color: #16a34a; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Key Insight</strong>
          EPDM is the dominant gasket material in HFA-propelled pharmaceutical MDI valves because HFA-134a and HFA-227ea are non-polar fluorocarbon propellants with very low solvating power for most elastomers — EPDM swells minimally, maintaining its sealing geometry and metering chamber volume over the entire product shelf life.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="section-6" class="section">
        <h2>MDV vs Continuous Spray Valves: Technical Comparison</h2>
        <p>The choice between a metered dose valve and a continuous spray valve (CSV) is determined by the dose precision requirement of the product, not merely the intended application. Many aerosol products that appear to require precision — such as hair sprays or personal care mists — actually use continuous spray valves, because their performance depends on applied quantity being controlled by user judgement rather than mechanical metering. Conversely, pharmaceutical aerosols require metered dose valves as a non-negotiable design requirement because drug delivery safety depends on each actuation delivering a consistent pharmacological dose.</p>

        <p>For a comprehensive technical grounding in how different propellant systems interact with valve type selection, our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/how-aerosol-cans-work" target="_top">how aerosol cans work</a> provides the foundational context. The propellant's vapour pressure, its miscibility with the concentrate, and whether the formulation is a solution or suspension all affect which valve architecture delivers optimal performance. This is also relevant when considering the full aerosol manufacturing process as described in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" target="_top">Aerosol Manufacturing 101 guide</a>.</p>

