      <!-- Section 7 -->
      <section id="section-7" class="section">
        <h2>Testing &amp; Quality Control for Metered Dose Valves</h2>
        <p>Quality control of metered dose valve performance spans the entire product lifecycle — from incoming component inspection, through in-process filling line checks, to finished product release testing and ongoing stability monitoring. The high precision required of MDVs means that quality failures at any stage can result in products with dose accuracy or leak performance outside specification, with potentially serious consequences in pharmaceutical applications. In consumer aerosol markets, valve QC failures manifest as user complaints about inconsistent spray output, premature emptying, or leaking products.</p>

        <p>The <a href="https://www.bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">British Aerosol Manufacturers' Association (BAMA)</a> publishes technical guidance on aerosol quality control practices that includes recommended sampling frequencies, acceptance criteria, and test methods for valve performance at both component receipt and finished goods stages. Their guidance is aligned with the EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive requirements and provides a practical framework applicable to manufacturers serving European and international markets.</p>

        <p>Key QC tests for metered dose valves include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Actuation force testing:</strong> Measures the force required to actuate the valve stem. Excessive actuation force indicates spring rate drift, gasket swelling, or stem/body interference. Insufficient force suggests spring fatigue or loss of sealing contact.</li>
          <li><strong>Spray weight / dose weight per actuation:</strong> Gravimetric measurement of delivered mass per actuation across a defined number of shots. This is the primary functional measure of dose accuracy for both pharmaceutical and consumer aerosol products.</li>
          <li><strong>Leak rate testing:</strong> Hot water bath immersion per EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive; gravimetric leak rate measurement (weight loss per unit time) for pharmaceutical products; electronic leak detection on high-speed filling lines using ultrasonic or pressure-decay methods.</li>
          <li><strong>Total number of metered shots:</strong> Verification that the canister delivers at least the labelled number of actuations, confirming metering chamber function and fill weight accuracy over the complete product life.</li>
          <li><strong>Valve crimp integrity:</strong> Dimensional inspection of the crimp geometry between valve cup and can opening — the primary seal against internal pressure loss outside the valve mechanism itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Gasket swell testing:</strong> Immersion of gasket specimens in the formulation and/or propellant for defined durations at elevated temperature, measuring dimensional change, weight change, and mechanical property retention. Per <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a>, the propellant's solubility parameter and polarity index are reliable indicators of elastomer swell risk prior to physical testing.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section id="faq" class="section" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <h2 style="font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            What is a metered dose valve in aerosol products?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">A metered dose valve (MDV) is a precision aerosol valve that delivers a fixed, reproducible quantity of product with each actuation. It achieves this through an internal metering chamber — a small cavity of defined volume that fills with formulation between actuations and empties completely upon each press. MDVs are essential in pharmaceutical inhalers, topical drug sprays, and any application where dose precision directly affects safety or efficacy.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            How does a metered dose valve achieve consistent dose output?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">Dose consistency in an MDV depends on three interacting factors: the precision-engineered volume of the metering chamber, the complete and repeatable filling and emptying cycle of that chamber, and the dimensional tolerances of the valve stem and body. Between actuations, the metering chamber refills with formulation through an inlet orifice; on actuation, the stem movement simultaneously closes the inlet and opens the outlet, expelling only the metered volume. Any variability in stem travel, gasket deformation, or chamber volume propagates directly into delivered dose variability.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            What standards govern aerosol valve leak testing?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">Aerosol valve leak performance is evaluated under several international frameworks. For pharmaceutical MDIs, ICH Q1A stability guidelines require leak rate assessment over the product shelf life. The EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive 75/324/EEC specifies maximum permissible leak rates for consumer aerosols. ASTM D3925 provides a standard test method for aerosol dispensers that covers leak detection during filling and finished product QC. Individual pharmacopoeial monographs (USP, Ph.Eur.) establish limits specifically for inhalation aerosol leakage.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            What materials are used for metered dose valve gaskets?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">Gasket material selection is one of the most critical decisions in MDV design, because the gasket must seal under pressure without swelling, hardening, or extracting materials into the formulation. The most common materials are EPDM (ethylene propylene diene monomer) rubber, Buna-N (nitrile), neoprene, and thermoplastic elastomers. For HFA-propelled pharmaceutical MDIs, specific elastomer grades are qualified under regulatory submissions because extractables and leachables from the gasket must meet toxicological thresholds documented in FDA and EMA guidance.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            How is aerosol valve dose accuracy tested?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">Dose accuracy testing for MDVs typically involves gravimetric methods — weighing the mass delivered per actuation using a precision balance — or volumetric collection into a calibrated vessel. For pharmaceutical inhalation products, dose uniformity is assessed using apparatus described in pharmacopoeial methods such as the Andersen Cascade Impactor. In industrial or consumer aerosol contexts, spray-weight tests per ASTM D5964 provide spray rate data used to infer valve consistency.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            What causes dose-to-dose variability in metered dose valves?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">Dose variability in MDVs arises from several interrelated sources. Incomplete metering chamber fill — caused by high-viscosity formulations, entrained propellant gas, or inadequate dwell time between actuations — directly reduces delivered dose. Gasket creep or swell under prolonged propellant contact alters the effective chamber volume. Stem travel inconsistency due to manufacturing tolerance variation changes the outlet orifice opening. Particle agglomeration in suspension formulations can partially block the inlet orifice, and operator actuation force and speed affect how fully the chamber is expelled.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: var(--surface);">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
            What is the difference between a metered dose valve and a continuous spray valve?
          </summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #374151; line-height: 1.65;">A continuous spray valve (CSV) delivers product for as long as the actuator is depressed, with no fixed per-actuation volume. A metered dose valve, by contrast, delivers only the pre-defined volume held in its metering chamber per actuation, regardless of how long the actuator is held down. CSVs are used in household, personal care, and industrial products where variable application is acceptable. MDVs are mandatory wherever a reproducible unit dose must be guaranteed — primarily pharmaceutical aerosols and precision technical spray applications.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

